[
    {
        "content": "<p>A design question. Any <code>SeminormedAddCommGroup</code> is also a <code>PseudoMetricSpace</code> and <code>dist x y = ‖x - y‖</code> holds  propositionally via <code>dist_eq_norm</code> but not definitionally transparent through <code>rfl</code>. Does that mean that <code>dist _ _</code> is always the correct and preferred way of referring to the distance? Is there ever a reason to use <code>‖_ - _‖</code> other than when extracting some distance from a norm in a proof and immediately <code>rw</code>ing it with <code>dist_eq_norm</code>?</p>\n<p>The thing that confuses me is that both patterns seem equally prevalent in mathlib: 1493  counts in 177 files and 1709  in 235 files if my math is correct. There are also explicit lemmas that just chain a <code>rw</code> and a <code>dist</code> lemma, e.g., <a href=\"https://github.com/FordUniver/mathlib4/blob/c94700f22c98857ce05af8bc0c46f6d6c6b2f504/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Function/ConvergenceInMeasure.lean#L119\">tendstoInMeasure_iff_norm</a>. The reason that I am asking is that I was curious about whether a PR for something as simple as the following is welcome and if so where it should live:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">to_additive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">norm_sub_le_diam_of_mem</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">norm_div_le_diam_of_mem'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsBounded</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hy</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">diam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simpa</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">only</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"bp\">←</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dist_eq_norm_div</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">using</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dist_le_diam_of_mem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hy</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 593491051,
        "sender_full_name": "Christoph Spiegel",
        "timestamp": 1778152423
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't think this lemma is a good lemma because the right hand side (<code>diam</code>) makes sense in a general metric space, so the general version of the lemma should be phrased with <code>dist</code> and then there is no reason to have a specific norm version. On the other hand, when you have something which only makes sense in normed spaces, then I think you can use <code>dist x y</code> or <code>||x - y||</code> interchangeably.</p>",
        "id": 593494093,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1778153265
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So if my statement lives in a metric space and no norm occurs, use <code>dist _ _</code>, otherwise <code>‖_ - _‖</code> is preferred (or acceptable)? And translation lemmas are appropriate in mathlib only if they naturally involve a norm that is not just expressing a <code>dist</code>? So the <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Function/ConvergenceInMeasure.html#MeasureTheory.tendstoInMeasure_iff_norm\">tendstoInMeasure_iff_norm</a> example I mentioned is then a bad or borderline one?</p>\n<p>For context: I am working on some gradient-based optimization guarantees, basic statements about Gradient Descent or Frank Wolfe on convex sets in Hilbert/Banach spaces. So here  using <code>‖x - y‖</code> in statements that have <code>[NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]</code> throughout and just converting with <code>dist_eq_norm</code> when connecting to the metric side is the correct approach?</p>",
        "id": 593508079,
        "sender_full_name": "Christoph Spiegel",
        "timestamp": 1778156613
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would prefer using <code>dist</code> even when there is a norm, since it makes it easier to apply lemmas about <code>dist</code></p>",
        "id": 593532324,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1778162189
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think it depends on the statement.</p>",
        "id": 593543142,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1778164571
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess the extreme option here would be to declare the dist spelling the simpNF</p>",
        "id": 593554668,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1778167234
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That would fly in the face of various simplifications related to <code>dist x 0</code>.</p>",
        "id": 593584360,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1778175064
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Let me mention that <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Real.norm_eq_abs#doc\">docs#Real.norm_eq_abs</a> is simp, which has sometimes bothered me.</p>",
        "id": 593586895,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1778175733
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To give a concrete example that I am currently working on, let's say I want to work with Lipschitz continuous gradients. That can simply be expressed as <code>LipschitzWith L (∇ f)</code> where </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">LipschitzWith</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">PseudoEMetricSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">PseudoEMetricSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NNReal</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">edist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">edist</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>in mathlib. But (at least for <code>β = ℝ</code>) there is the more general notion of Lipschitz Smoothness not currently in mathlib, which I would define as  </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LipschitzSmoothWith</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">NormedAddCommGroup</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">InnerProductSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CompleteSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">NNReal</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟪∇</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">⟫_</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">‖</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Assuming differentiable <code>f</code>, <code>LipschitzWith K (∇ f)</code> implies <code>LipschitzSmoothWith K f but the opposite direction additionally requires </code>(hconv : ConvexOn ℝ Set.univ f)`.</p>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> should <code>LipschitzSmoothWith</code> actually use <code>(e)dist x y</code> instead of <code>‖y - x‖</code>?  Or is the norm preferred here since we already have the inner product <code> ⟪_, y - x⟫_ℝ</code> in the statement?</p>",
        "id": 593727316,
        "sender_full_name": "Christoph Spiegel",
        "timestamp": 1778230891
    }
]